### [06:46:30 UTC] Step 2 — Hypothesize
Input: Cycle 6's _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak (single uniform per-channel scalar) verified to fix cross-band attenuation but (per this cycle's dispatch prompt, from audio-quality-checker) suppresses IST's own added detail almost everywhere.
Hypothesis: The peak inflation is driven by IST's own peak-relative thresholding privileging whichever frequency dominates the channel's spectrum (usually low-frequency content) plus that content's own spectral leakage/smearing (from WOLA per-block windowing) into neighboring bins -- NOT by genuinely separate quiet high-frequency content IST also adds. Splitting ist_changes's own FFT spectrum into a "dominant" band (bins >= a small ratio of the spectrum's own peak magnitude) and a "residual" band (everything else), then applying the existing iterative-shrink cap ONLY to the dominant band while leaving residual untouched, should keep the combined-peak-inflation fix intact while leaving quiet/non-dominant content (e.g. real added HF detail) essentially unscaled.
Prediction: On the existing adversarial synthetic test (loud 100 Hz + quiet 8000 Hz component), a frequency-selective cap will (a) still reduce combined-peak excess over baseline to well under 15% of the uncapped excess (matching/improving cycle 6's own bound), and (b) leave the 8000 Hz component's own magnitude in ist_changes close to 100% intact, versus cycle 6's ~5.9% survival on the same case. If wrong: either the peak reduction would fail to reach the <15% bound (meaning the "dominant vs residual" split doesn't actually isolate the real driver), or the 8kHz content would still get significantly reduced (meaning it isn't actually spectrally separable from the dominant band).

### [06:47:00 UTC] Step 3 — Experiment (spectral investigation, before editing source)
Input: Ran ad hoc CuPy scripts (scratchpad, not committed) against the existing (pre-fix) codebase using the exact synthetic case from test_uncapped_ist_inflates_combined_channel_peak.
Action: (1) Computed rfft of uncapped ist_changes; found the 100 Hz bin holds ~1.25e9 magnitude vs a narrow 8000 Hz band holding only ~2e5 (a ~4 order-of-magnitude gap), confirming clean spectral separability for this synthetic case. (2) Found the "residual" region (everything below a naive single-bin/high-ratio dominant mask) is dominated by spectral leakage of the 100 Hz tone spread over 0-1000 Hz (from per-block WOLA windowing), NOT genuine broadband content -- a single-bin dominant mask alone gave only a ~15.3%-of-uncapped-excess reduction (borderline against the 15% bound), refuting a naive "just the single peak bin" design. (3) Swept dominant_band_ratio from 0.5 down to 0.0001: excess-ratio improved monotonically (0.5->15.3%, 0.01->13.9%, 0.002->8.0%, 0.0001->5.9%) while 8kHz survival stayed at 100% across the ENTIRE sweep (the 8kHz bin's own magnitude, ~5e-5 of peak, sits far below even the smallest ratio tested) -- confirming the hypothesis and giving comfortable margin to choose ratio=0.002 as a safety-conscious default. (4) Prototyped a layered design (frequency-selective pass + a safety-net whole-signal uniform-shrink fallback, only engaged if the frequency-selective candidate's own combined peak still exceeds baseline_peak*1.2) and confirmed: normal case (ratio=0.002) -> 8.0% excess ratio, 100% 8kHz survival, safety net inactive; pathological case (dominant_band_ratio=1.5, empty dominant mask) -> safety net correctly engages and reproduces cycle 6's own ~5.9% excess ratio (i.e. never regresses below the old guarantee); both pre-existing no-op cases (zero baseline, out-of-phase no-cap-needed) still return ist_changes unmodified/allclose.
Reasoning: Needed to verify the mechanism empirically (not just assume it) before committing to source changes, since a naive frequency split could easily fail to bound the peak (if the "driver" content turned out to be broadband) or fail to preserve HF detail (if the two bands weren't actually separable in magnitude).
Output: Design confirmed as sound and safely bounded; proceeded to implement in source.

### [06:52:00 UTC] Step 4 — Implement fix
Input: Confirmed design from Step 3.
Action: Rewrote _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak in fat_llama/audio_fattener/feed.py: splits ist_changes's rfft spectrum into "dominant" (magnitude >= dominant_band_ratio=0.002 * spectrum peak) and "residual" components; iteratively shrinks only the dominant component via the same bounded multiplicative-shrink method as cycle 6; falls back to one additional whole-signal uniform-shrink pass (cycle 6's original method) only if the frequency-selective candidate's own combined peak still exceeds baseline_peak*safety_margin=1.2. Updated docstring to document the cycle 6->7 history, the empirical basis for the two new parameters, and the remaining (documented, same as cycle 6) non-guarantee. Updated upscale_channels' docstring reference accordingly. No change to iterative_soft_thresholding itself or any other DSP stage.
Reasoning: Matches the confirmed hypothesis/design; keeps the change scoped to the one function plus its caller's docstring.
